Buying a home in the Capital City usually starts with one question: what can I realistically afford? Montgomery offers a range of housing, from older neighborhoods near downtown to newer subdivisions in the east, and the right financing can matter as much as the house itself.
This post explains how affordable mortgage loans work, which loan types are worth a look, and what to compare before you commit. It's written for buyers who want plain answers, not sales language.

What "Affordable" Actually Means

An affordable mortgage isn't just one with a low advertised rate. It's a loan whose total cost fits comfortably in your budget over time. That includes:
  
    • The interest rate and APR. The APR reflects the rate plus certain lender fees, so it gives a fuller picture of borrowing costs. 
    • Your down payment. Some programs allow far less than the traditional 20%. 
    • Closing costs. These typically include lender fees, title services, and prepaid items like homeowners insurance. 
    • Mortgage insurance. Loans with smaller down payments often come with monthly or upfront insurance charges. 
    • Property taxes and homeowners insurance. These are usually folded into your monthly payment through escrow. 
A good rule of thumb is to look at the full monthly payment, not only principal and interest.

Loan Types Worth Considering in Montgomery

FHA Loans
Insured by the Federal Housing Administration, FHA loans are designed for buyers who may have a smaller down payment or a less-than-perfect credit history. They're popular with first-time buyers, though they do require mortgage insurance premiums.

VA Loans
Montgomery is home to Maxwell Air Force Base and Gunter Annex, so VA loans are especially relevant here. Backed by the U.S. Department of Veterans Affairs, these loans are available to eligible service members, veterans, and some surviving spouses. They often allow no down payment and don't require monthly mortgage insurance, though a funding fee may apply.

USDA Loans
Buyers looking at homes outside the city center, in eligible rural or suburban areas, may qualify for USDA loans. Eligibility depends on both the property location and household income, so it's worth checking the USDA's property map before you start shopping.
Conventional Loans
Not backed by a federal agency, conventional loans can work well for buyers with solid credit and a moderate down payment. With less than 20% down, you'll generally pay private mortgage insurance, but it can often be removed once you've built enough equity.

Help With Down Payments and Closing Costs
Many buyers assume they need a large pile of cash to get started, but assistance programs can change that math. State and local housing finance agencies, including the Alabama Housing Finance Authority, have offered programs aimed at first-time and moderate-income buyers. Program details, income limits, and availability change, so confirm the current terms directly with the agency or a lender who works with them.

Steps to Keep Your Costs Down
    1. Check your credit reports first. You can pull free reports at AnnualCreditReport.com. Fixing errors before you apply can improve the terms you're offered. 
    2. Get pre-approved. A pre-approval shows sellers you're serious and tells you a realistic price range. 
    3. Compare multiple lenders. Rates and fees vary, and the Consumer Financial Protection Bureau encourages shoppers to compare offers. 
    4. Review your Loan Estimate. Lenders are required to provide this standardized form within three business days of your application, making side-by-side comparisons much easier. 
    5. Budget beyond the payment. Maintenance, utilities, and insurance all belong in your monthly plan. 

